    "group": "101-200",
    "title": "Palindrome Partitioning II",
    "workload": "four 500-character case strings built once over alphabets of size 2, 4, 26 and 3, then 400 punches of the full O(n^2) DP. Alphabet size is the knob: a 2-letter alphabet makes the palindrome table dense so the cut loop scans many true entries, while 26 letters make almost nothing a palindrome and the inner loop exits early -- the set spans both so neither branch of the pal[0][i] test dominates. This is the DP sibling of #131: that kata enumerates every partitioning and is dominated by per-piece String allocation, while this one only needs the minimum cut count, so a table replaces the backtracking and the cost becomes a flat O(n^2) fill with no allocation in the inner loops. Benching both separates kara on String churn from kara on a dense 2-D table. NOTE: all lanes use a genuinely nested bool table (row pointers / Vec<Vec<bool>> / [][]bool), not a flat block or a packed bitset.",
